## 3. The known residual (one-direction drift) — probed, and I concur

The brief accepts: a step removed from CI_STEPS leaves its text behind in the
"Today those are" sentence, and no documentation assertion rejects that.

Probed directly ($TMPDIR/residual.mjs, same restore discipline):

  R1  remove `npm run -s lint` from CI_STEPS only
      exit 1 — FAIL  gates job's run: steps equal CI_STEPS minus the studio check, in order
      (Matches the brief's claim that removal fails the existing comparison.)

  R2  coordinated removal: drop lint from CI_STEPS AND from ci.yml's gates job,
      leave docs untouched
      exit 1 — FAIL  has a lint step
      docs still claim `npm run -s lint` is a step: true

So the residual is NARROWER than the architect credited. The guard's
pre-existing per-step assertions ("has a typecheck/lint/format:check/test
step", "has a check --repo . step naming studio", "... naming
examples/sample-studio") hardcode all six CI_STEPS entries against ci.yml, so
a coordinated two-file removal is still caught incidentally. The stale doc
text survives uncaught only on a THREE-place edit that also removes the
hardcoded assertion from this very script — and whoever is editing that line
is sitting three lines above the doc assertions.

I CONCUR with the acceptance. Closing it honestly would mean asserting that
the "Today those are" prose list equals CI_STEPS exactly, which means parsing
an English sentence; that is a more brittle guard than the gap it closes, and
it would fight the section's own prose ("Today those are ... and ...").
Not worth an opus. Recorded here so the next reader does not re-derive it.

