## 8. The provenance transcript — my judgment

The brief (Acceptance, "Render parity") designates the builder's step-3
transcript as "the only provenance evidence" that CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md came
from the renderer rather than a byte-identical hand edit.

What was delivered (/tmp/claude-1000/w050-builder-report.md:10-19):

     Verbatim `instructions --write` step output (`instructions --write` itself was silent):
      M AGENTS.md
      M CLAUDE.md
      M packages/cli/src/instructions.template.md
     1	0	AGENTS.md
     1	0	CLAUDE.md
     1	0	packages/cli/src/instructions.template.md

It does NOT satisfy me as provenance, for two reasons, and the second is the
one that matters:

  (a) It is a reconstruction, not a capture. There is no prompt, no command
      echo, no interleaving; the `instructions --write` invocation appears only
      as a parenthetical assertion that it printed nothing. The brief asked for
      the transcript "from the `instructions --write` command through the
      `git diff --numstat` output". What arrived is the concatenated stdout of
      two later commands.

  (b) Even a perfect terminal capture of these two commands could not make the
      discrimination it was assigned. `git status` and `git diff --numstat`
      AFTER `--write` are identical whether the renderer wrote the two files or
      a hand edit did. The one observation that would discriminate is a
      `git status --porcelain` taken BETWEEN the template edit and the
      `--write` run, showing ONLY the template modified — one line, and it is
      absent from the brief's step 3.

I do not fail the opus on this, and I want the reasoning on the record:

  - The artifact is verifiably correct independent of provenance. I re-ran
    `instructions --studio studio --repo . --write` myself at 233668a and got a
    zero diff (section 1). The committed bytes ARE the render.
  - Divergence cannot persist: the `instructions.stale` check rule re-derives
    this on every check run, and the studio check is a CI_STEPS entry. A hand
    edit that matched byte-for-byte is indistinguishable from the render
    because it IS the render; a hand edit that did not match would have been
    caught by that rule before this review.
  - The brief's own text concedes render parity "cannot prove *how* they were
    produced". The transcript was the attempt to close that residual, and it
    does not close it. The gap is the brief's, not the builder's: Sol produced
    what step 8 asked for, in the form step 8 asked for it.

OBSERVATION O1 (single instance, for the retro, not a defect in this opus):
  An evidence artifact was specified that cannot bear the weight assigned to
  it — "the only provenance evidence" is a self-reported after-the-fact
  transcript of two commands whose output is provenance-blind. The fix is one
  line in the next brief of this shape: require `git status --porcelain`
  BEFORE the `--write` run, showing only the template modified.
  Searched for other instances: grepped all of studio/briefs/*.md for
  "transcript" and for "provenance". W-050 is the only brief that designates a
  builder-reported transcript as evidence; W-044:243 puts a transcript in
  review evidence (the censor's own, not the builder's), and the "provenance"
  hits in W-018/W-033/W-034/W-039/W-041/W-049 are the decisions/D-nnn.md
  front-matter field or W-044's unrelated round-3 fault. NOT a class — one
  instance.

